won/lost; step5 generates the report). `price_position_pct` =
`(our_bid - winner_price) / winner_price * 100` with `Decimal`, stored half-up
2dp like every other number in the repo.

## 4. Stage 2 - The "why" analysis (step2_why.py)

Pure function over `calc.json` + `outcome.json` -> `analysis.json`. No ML: typed
factors with explicit values, so the first post-mortems work with zero training
data. Factors and where they come from:

| Factor | Computation | Source field |
|---|---|---|
| `price_position` | our_bid vs winner price, absolute + pct | outcome |
| `missing_quotes` | count and cost share of `status == "supplier_quote_required"` positions; supplier-package material runs at budget rates, so the share of `direct_position_cost_chf` flagged `price_source == "supplier_package"` estimates the pricing risk we carried | `calc.position_results` |
| `match_quality` | value-weighted mean `match_confidence` (0.72 supplier vs 0.92 catalog today; real scores once `_1` provides them) + count `review_required` | `calc.position_results` |
| `cost_structure` | material / labour / services / allowances shares of `project_cost` | `calc.project_cost` |
| `scenario_gap` | which margin scenario was submitted vs the aggressive one; price delta to winner at the aggressive margin (bridges to `_3_Prediction`'s Aggressive/Target/Conservative slider) | outcome + `project_parameters.json` |
| `labour_pressure` | hours vs budget if productivity factors were manual (were we slow?) | `calc` + settings `productivity` |

Verdict rule (first pass, deterministic): rank factors, pick the top actionable
one, e.g. `price_over_by_8.9pct_with_22pct_unquoted_supplier_share -> likely price,
amplified by quote risk`. Each analysis records `factors`, `verdict`,
`confidence` (`low/medium/high` from how complete the outcome data was), and the
full input hashes (`calc_sha`, `outcome_sha`) for traceability - the repo's
"every result traceable to its source file" convention.

