## 10. Risks

- **Sparse labels**: months may pass between tenders; a LoRA trained on <30
  outcomes will overfit. Mitigation: keep the deterministic step2 factors as the
  always-on baseline; LoRA is additive, and the switch gate requires a minimum n.
- **Label noise**: "lost" is not always price (incumbency, framework contracts);
  the `notes`/verdict fields keep human context; confidence tiers propagate.
- **Circularity**: prices were set using the model now being trained on their
  outcomes (feedback bias). Mitigation: store `win_estimate_pct` and scenario at
  submission; treat evaluation on out-of-time splits only.
- **Public data availability**: award notices do not always publish competitor
  prices; `info_gathering: partial/failed` and the report path are first-class,
  not exceptions.
- **Catalog auto-update**: wrong auto-applied prices silently corrupt future
  calcs; auto-apply only price deltas within threshold, everything else pending
  review (step3), and never rewrite `materials/` directly.
- **Base-model churn**: switching base model orphans LoRA versions (already
  handled by `_norm_settings`) - the fallback heuristic must be implemented and
  visible, not a silent None.
- **Privacy**: competitor pricing is commercially sensitive; keep tenders/ and
  observations/ gitignored (already the pattern for `tenders/`, `uploads/`).

